# Docker does NOT read .gitignore. Without this file, the Dockerfile's `COPY . .` bakes every
# .env file -- including the one holding live OpenAI/Anthropic/Mistral/Tavily/LangSmith keys --
# plus .git and .venv into an image layer, where `docker history` reads them straight back out.
# Bible Step 16 DoD: "Zero secrets in image or env dumps."
#
# **Default-deny.** Everything is excluded, then runtime needs are added back. That direction is
# deliberate: a new secret file added to the repo next month is excluded automatically, whereas a
# denylist would ship it silently. The cost is that a forgotten *runtime* file breaks the build --
# a loud failure, which is the trade you want from a control whose job is preventing a quiet leak.
*

# --- what the runtime actually needs ---
!app
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
!docker-entrypoint.sh
!migrations
!alembic.ini

# migrations/ + alembic.ini ship in the image on purpose: `alembic upgrade head` is run from this
# same image as a deploy-time job, so the schema and the code that expects it are always the same
# build. Running migrations from a separately-built artifact is how the two drift apart.

# --- junk that lives *inside* the paths re-included above ---
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo

# Note: no .env file reaches the image, by design. Bible Step 1 -- ".env is dev-only; prod secrets
# go to Azure Key Vault (Step 16)." Compose injects .env.development as real environment variables
# rather than as a file, and config.py skips absent .env files silently while real process env vars
# take highest precedence -- so this works out of the box in both places.
